            <ArticleTitle>Body mass index mediates the prognostic significance of circulating tumor cells in inflammatory breast cancer.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>666-671</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0002-9610(17)30118-6</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.amjsurg.2017.06.005</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Obesity (BMI≥30) may be an etiologic and prognostic factor in inflammatory breast cancer (IBC). We examined the relationship between BMI, pathologic complete response (pCR), and circulating-tumor-cell (CTC) levels in IBC.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Cohort included IBC patients diagnosed 2005-2015 who had neoadjuvant chemotherapy during a prospective trial on CTCs and pathologic review describing pCR. Chi-square, logistic regression, and Cox proportional hazards models were used to identify clinicopathologic associations with event-free survival (EFS).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Of 73 patients, 61 (84%) had CTC values, 22 (30%) achieved a pCR, and 39 (53%) were obese. There was no difference between obese and non-obese patients for pCR rates (31% vs. 29%, p = 0.90) or presence of CTCs (23% vs. 26%, p = 0.80). Among non-obese patients, CTCs were associated with worse EFS (HR 11.69, p &lt; 0.01), but among obese patients, there was no difference in EFS between those with and without CTCs.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">BMI mediates CTCs' prognostic significance in IBC.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Copyright © 2017. Published by Elsevier Inc.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
